{
	tags: vector, logical,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	Remember that if the input is &lt;pf&gt;30&lt;/pf&gt;, this program returns &lt;pf&gt;[5, 6]&lt;/pf&gt;, not &lt;pf&gt;[6, 5]&lt;/pf&gt;.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>number</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>pair</pf> (1 x 2) double vector — row vector containing the pair of integers that make <pf>number</pf> oblong.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>If you think logically about this problem, you can write it without a loop.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		oblong_pair(56)
		7  8
		oblong_pair(99)
		[]
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
